<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All Week Long! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 0.9em; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Tuesday!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Reading: Find familiar words in a text.<br>
            Reading: Identify one-step written directions with visual support.<br>
            Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Reading: Can find and point to familiar words in simple texts (e.g., "Monday," "Tuesday").<br>
            Can identify and follow one-step written directions with visual support (e.g., following the instruction, "Point to the word 'Monday'" in the sentence "Today is Monday," supported by a calendar picture).<br>
            Can identify the sounds of the letters of the alphabet in familiar vocabulary related to days of the week and daily routines when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound (e.g., identifying the sound /w/ in "Wednesday" supported with a picture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>
                Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts the class by greeting students with "Good morning, everyone! What day is today?" The teacher shows a large flashcard with "TUESDAY" written on it and a picture representing a school day. Students repeat "Today is Tuesday!" chorally. The teacher then leads a quick "Days of the Week" song, pointing to individual word flashcards (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, etc.) as they are sung.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large sentence strip that reads: "Today is Tuesday." The teacher points to each word as they say it slowly: "Today... is... Tuesday." The teacher then specifically points to the word "Tuesday" and says, "Look! This says 'Tuesday'." The teacher shows a simple calendar drawing and points to the word "Tuesday" on it, reinforcing the visual connection.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding of the written words.<br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'Tuesday') "Is this 'Monday' or 'Tuesday'?" (Students respond: 'Tuesday' or point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'today' in a sentence) "Can you find 'day'?" (Students point to 'day').</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also models the sound /d/ in "day" by clearly enunciating it and asking students to repeat the sound.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
                Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful "Mini-Calendar Page" (a visual aid) with a simple text for the week. The text will feature a picture for each day and the corresponding written word and simple sentences. Example:
                "Monday: School Day!
                Tuesday: School Day! Today is Tuesday.
                Wednesday: Busy Day!
                ...
                Saturday: Weekend Fun!
                Sunday: Relaxing Day!"
                The text is simple, uses "Noun + verb + noun" (e.g., "Today is Tuesday."), and present simple (e.g., "It is the weekend.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using time-related vocabulary: Identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to "Today is Tuesday." on the Mini-Calendar Page and asks, "What day is today?" (Students respond "Tuesday" or point to the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Find the word 'Monday'." Students point to 'Monday' on the calendar page.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Point to 'weekend'." Students point to 'Saturday' and 'Sunday'.</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also asks students to listen for and identify the /d/ sound in "day" as they read the words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
                Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Find familiar words in a text. Reading: Identify one-step written directions with visual support. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of letters (e.g., /d/ in 'day').<br>
                <b>Grammar:</b> Noun + verb + noun (e.g., "Today is Monday.").<br>
                <b>Vocabulary:</b> Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, day, today, weekend.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Word Matching & Circling):</b> Students receive a worksheet with two columns. Column A has pictures representing different days (e.g., a child studying for "Tuesday," a family picnicking for "Sunday"). Column B has the written words (Monday, Tuesday, Sunday, etc.). Students draw a line from the picture to the correct day word. Below, there's a small section of text: "Today is Tuesday. It is a school day." Students are instructed: "Circle the word 'Tuesday'." The teacher explicitly asks, "What sound do you hear at the start of 'day'?" to reinforce /d/ sound.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Read and Follow):</b> The teacher displays a series of large sentence strips one by one, for example: "Today is Monday." then "Today is Tuesday." Students identify and say the day of the week. Then, the teacher gives a simple written direction on a separate card, like: "Point to the word 'today' in the sentence." or "Underline 'Tuesday'." Students follow the instruction on their worksheets or point to the word on the displayed strip.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Point):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small sentence card, for example, "Today is Tuesday. Tomorrow is Wednesday." Partner A reads a familiar word (e.g., "Tuesday") and Partner B points to it on the card. Then they switch roles. This encourages using simple phrases for identifying words and active reading engagement.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
                Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Week's Reading Adventure" - Students engage in a "reading hunt" around the classroom.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places various cards around the classroom, each with a short, simple sentence related to the days of the week and a single, one-step written direction. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li>Card 1: "Today is Tuesday. Circle 'Tuesday'." (with a picture of a school desk)</li>
                            <li>Card 2: "Yesterday was Monday. Point to 'Monday'." (with a picture of a sun)</li>
                            <li>Card 3: "Tomorrow is Wednesday. Underline 'Wednesday'." (with a picture of a book)</li>
                            <li>Card 4: "It is the weekend. Draw a star next to 'weekend'." (with a picture of children playing)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are given a checklist or response sheet. In small groups or individually, they walk around the classroom, find the cards, read the sentences, and follow the one-step written direction on each card (e.g., circle, point, underline, draw) directly on the card using a pencil or marker.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes students as they identify familiar words and execute the directions, providing support as needed.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
                Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Reading Hunt" and an individual exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive an exit ticket with a short text and two written directions.<br>
                Example text: "Today is Tuesday. It is a busy day. Monday was yesterday."<br>
                Directions:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Circle the word 'Tuesday'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Point to the word 'day'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Draw a square around 'Monday'.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can find and point to familiar words (e.g., 'Tuesday', 'day', 'Monday').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow one-step written directions (e.g., circle, point, draw a square).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify the sound /d/ in "day" when prompted.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> On the back of the assessment ticket, students draw a happy face or a sad face for each statement:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the word 'Tuesday'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow the direction to 'circle' a word."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to read today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult to read today?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The use of large print and clear visuals (calendar, pictures) worked very well for 1st graders to find familiar words. TPR for following directions was also effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with quickly differentiating similar-looking words (e.g., 'today' and 'Tuesday'). More direct practice on initial letter sounds might be beneficial.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For the next lesson, I will ensure more explicit modeling of word recognition strategies and perhaps a short matching game for similar words.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading lesson provided a solid foundation for recognizing the written words for days of the week. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use these familiar words to actively speak and ask/answer questions about the days of the week, building on their reading comprehension by expressing what they have read.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>